course: everyday
lesson: 400
title: What the numbers on the box mean
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In the shop Kai pointed at a big red 18 on a game box. «Does that mean it takes eighteen hours?» His mum laughed so loudly that a man in the next aisle turned round. She explained: it is an age rating. PEGI 18 means the game is for adults, because of what is in it, not how long it lasts or how hard it is. Ruby made the opposite mistake: she refused a PEGI 7 game as babyish, and it turned out to be a fiendish puzzle game that beat her dad.
explain: Different countries use different labels. In the UK and most of Europe it is PEGI, with the numbers 3, 7, 12, 16 and 18. Priya's uncle in America sent a photo of the same game with ESRB M on it, which means Mature, for about 17 and up. Two systems, one idea. When Theo told the class PEGI stands for «Pretty Evil Games Inside», everyone laughed, and Mrs Okafor laughed too, then wrote the real name on the board: Pan European Game Information. Nobody ever forgot it, thanks to Theo.
explain: Noah said ratings were just to stop kids having fun. His dad turned the box over and showed him the little pictures that say why a game got its number: a fist for violence, a speech bubble for bad language, a spider for scary content. A rating is not random; it tells you what is inside. Zara asked why a cartoon game with blood was 16 when a film with blood was 12. Her mum said that in a game you are the one doing it, again and again, and that changes how it feels.
explain: Ollie's 15-year-old cousin said he could play 18 games because he was «basically 18». His aunt said 15 is still 15, and the cousin went red. Jayden found it annoying that his mum checked every rating, and felt babied, as if he were the only one, until he discovered Leo's parents checked too; Leo just never said. Mia's console had a parental control setting that blocked games above 12, and her parents promised to change it as she got older and showed her how it worked.
explain: Leo, invited to play a horror game, said «It's rated 16, I'm not allowed» in a very relieved voice. Later he admitted he simply did not like scary things. His friends happily played something else. You never need a rule to say no to something you do not like.
fact: The number on a game box is an age rating: it says who the game is suitable for, not how long or hard it is.
fact: PEGI, which stands for Pan European Game Information, is used in the UK, Ireland and most of Europe.
fact: The PEGI ages are 3, 7, 12, 16 and 18.
fact: In the USA and Canada games are rated by the ESRB; M means Mature, suitable for about 17 and up.
fact: Small pictures on the box, such as a fist for violence or a spider for fear, show why a game got its rating.
fact: A low rating means suitable for younger players; it does not mean easy, boring or babyish.
fact: Age ratings count your real age, not how grown-up you feel.
fact: In a game the player does the actions over and over, which is one reason games can be rated differently from films.
fact: Most consoles have parental controls that can block games above a chosen age rating.
fact: Many families check game ratings, even if children do not talk about it.
fact: You can say «I don't want to play that» without needing a rule as an excuse.
exercise: What does the 18 on Kai's game box mean? | The game is for adults because of what is in it | The game takes about eighteen hours to finish properly | The game has eighteen levels | The game costs eighteen pounds
exercise: How did Kai feel when his mum laughed? | Confused and a bit silly | Proud of his clever guess | Angry with the man in the aisle | Scared of the picture on the game box
exercise: Why was Ruby wrong to call the PEGI 7 game babyish? | The number shows who may play, not how hard it is | PEGI 7 games are always the hardest ones of all to beat | Babies are not allowed to play PEGI 7 games | Her dad had secretly changed the rating
exercise: What does PEGI stand for? | Pan European Game Information | Pretty Evil Games Inside | Players Enjoy Games Indoors | Parents Explain Games Instead
exercise: Why did nobody forget the real name of PEGI? | Theo's funny wrong answer made it stick | Mrs Okafor made them copy it fifty times | It was printed on their school jumpers | They had a test on it every day
exercise: Priya's uncle in America saw ESRB M. What does M mean? | Mature, for about 17 and up | Medium, for any player who likes a challenge | Monster, a scary game | Mini, for very young kids
exercise: What did Noah's dad show him to explain ratings? | The pictures saying what's inside | The price printed on the back of the box | The number of players allowed at once | A video of the hardest level
exercise: What does a fist picture on a game box warn about? | Violence | Bad language | Scary content | Online chat
exercise: Why can a game with blood get a higher rating than a film with blood? | In the game you are the one doing it, again and again | Games are always much longer than films, so you see more | Films are watched only by grown-ups | Game blood looks more real than film blood
exercise: Ollie's 15-year-old cousin said he was «basically 18». What did his aunt say? | 15 is still 15 | That is close enough to play | 18 games are fine at 15 | Only birthdays in summer count
exercise: How did Jayden feel about his mum checking every rating? | Babied, as if he was the only one | Glad, because he loves rules | Proud that she trusted him | Bored, because he never asked for games
exercise: What did Jayden find out about Leo's family? | They checked ratings too, Leo just never said | They let Leo play any game at all, even 18s | They had never heard of ratings | They only bought PEGI 3 games
exercise: What can a console's parental control setting do? | Block games above a chosen age rating | Make every game easier to win | Stop the console getting too hot | Turn the console off at bedtime only
exercise: Why did Mia's parents' promise make the setting feel fairer? | The rule would grow with her | They would turn it off for parties | They would let her choose the number | The setting would break by itself
exercise: Why did Leo use the rating to get out of the horror game? | He did not like scary things and wanted a way out | He wanted to report all his friends to their parents | He thought rules are more important than fun | He had already finished that horror game
exercise: What is the lesson from Leo's horror game story? | You can say no to a game without needing a rule | You should always find a rule to hide behind | Scary games are fine if friends want them | You must play whatever your friends pick
test: «big 18 on it, that's like 18 hours of gameplay, bargain». What's true? | It's the age it's meant for, not the hours | Yeah, the number is always how many hours it lasts | It means 18 levels to beat | It's the price in dollars
test: «PEGI 7?? that's for babies, no way». What's the mistake? | Low rating means suitable, not easy or babyish | PEGI 7 games are only for age seven exactly | Babies can't hold controllers anyway | The higher the number, the better the game
test: Theo: «PEGI means Pretty Evil Games Inside, look it up». What's the real name? | Pan European Game Information | Pretty Evil Games Inside | Public Entertainment Game Index | Players' European Gaming Institute
test: «my cousin's 15, says he's basically 18 so he can play 18s». Right? | No, ratings count real years | Yes, close enough counts | Yes, if he's tall for his age | Only if his mates say so
test: A boy in America reads «M» on a game. What age does that mean, about? | 17 and up | Seven and up | Twelve and up | Any age at all
test: «ratings are just grown-ups being bossy». Noah's dad turns the box over. What does he point to? | The pictures saying what's inside | The barcode and the price on the back | The shop's own sticker on the front | The name of the company
test: Zara: «why's a cartoon game with blood 16 and a film 12, makes no sense». Best answer? | In the game you do it yourself, over and over | Cartoons are scarier than films, everyone knows that | Films never have any blood | Ratings are picked at random
test: «u can't play anything over 12?? ur parents are so strict». What did Jayden find out? | Lots of families check | Only his parents ever check, nobody else's | Strict parents like his are breaking the law | Everyone else plays 18s every day
test: «it's PEGI 16, I'm not allowed, soz» — Leo sounds very relieved. What does the lesson say? | You can just say you don't want to play it | He should play it anyway to look brave | Rules are the only good reason to say no | He should check if it's really 16
test: Mia's console won't open a 16 game. «it's broken!!» What's really going on? | Parental controls block games above 12 | The disc must be scratched and need cleaning | The console hates new games | The fan is too loud again
test: Teacher: «A spider picture on a game box. What's it warning about?» | Scary content | Bad language | Violence | Online buying
exam: Identify the principal meaning of the numeral printed upon the packaging of a video game, such as the figure 18. | The minimum age for which the content is considered suitable | The duration of the game expressed in hours of play | The quantity of levels contained therein | The retail price of the product in pounds sterling, before any discount
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a game bearing the classification PEGI 7? | It is suitable for players of seven years and above, irrespective of difficulty | It is of necessity simple and intended for infants | It is to be played by children aged precisely seven and by no others | It is unsuitable for persons above seven years of age
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the reason for which a game depicting blood may receive a higher classification than a film of comparable content. | The player performs the actions personally and repeatedly | Films are invariably viewed in the company of adults | Games are of greater duration than any film | Classifications of games are assigned arbitrarily
exam: It is given that a person aged fifteen years asserts eligibility to play games classified 18 on the ground of maturity. State the correct position. | Classifications are determined by actual age, not perceived maturity | Maturity of character supersedes the stated classification | A person within three years of the age is eligible | Eligibility is determined by the opinion of peers
